--TEST--
Core\Jwt::sign takes a Core\Crypto\KeyPair beside a shared secret and the header's `alg` is the one algorithm that key has — HS256, ES256 and EdDSA, read back off the headers themselves — while an X25519 pair signs nothing and Core\Jwt::verify, which is HS256 alone, reads none of the asymmetric tokens — `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement over the keys that sign. One question — "what did the header say
// `alg` was?" — is asked of every kind of key this member takes, so a member
// that picked its algorithm from anywhere but the key fails here while still
// looking right on any single line. There is no `alg` argument to get wrong,
// which is the point: the key in slot 3 is the whole of the choice.
//
// The two RSA kinds are not in this sweep because there is no way to reach one
// from source without a PKCS#8 file, and the same table's RSA rows are pinned
// beside the function that answers them, in `jwt.rs`'s own
// `each_kind_carries_one_algorithm_and_x25519_carries_none`.
secret bytes $shared = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $p256 = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256);
Core\Crypto\KeyPair $ed25519 = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);

array<string> $tokens = [
    Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $shared),
    Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $p256),
    Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada"], 15m, $ed25519),
];

foreach ($tokens as string $token) {
    array<string> $parts = Core\Str::split($token, ".");
    echo Core\Encoding::decodeText(
        Core\Encoding::fromBase64Url($parts[0]),
        Core\Charset::Utf8
    ), "\n";
}

// The one kind of pair that is not a signing key at all. It is a bug rather
// than a verdict, because the program handed over a key for `Core\Crypto::agree`
// and nothing about a token is wrong.
try {
    Core\Jwt::sign(
        ["sub" => "ada"],
        15m,
        Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::X25519)
    );
    echo "an X25519 pair signed a token", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $signsNothing) {
    echo $signsNothing->message, "\n";
}

// And the read half did not widen with the write half. `Core\Jwt::verify` takes
// a shared secret and compares `alg` against HS256, so an asymmetric token is a
// token it refuses like any other — the same one sentence, not a new failure.
array<string> $asymmetric = [$tokens[1], $tokens[2]];
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($asymmetric as string $token) {
    try {
        Core\Jwt::verify($token, $shared);
    } catch (RuntimeError $notThisKeys) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "asymmetric tokens a shared-key verifier reads ", 2 - $refused, " of 2", "\n";

array<tainted string> $claims = Core\Jwt::verify($tokens[0], $shared);
echo "and the shared-key token still round-trips, sub ", $claims["sub"], "\n";
--EXPECT--
{"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}
{"alg":"ES256","typ":"JWT"}
{"alg":"EdDSA","typ":"JWT"}
Core\Jwt::sign(): an `X25519` pair signs nothing — it is a key for Core\Crypto::agree alone. A `P256` pair signs ES256, an `Ed25519` pair EdDSA, and the two RSA kinds RS256 and PS256.
asymmetric tokens a shared-key verifier reads 0 of 2
and the shared-key token still round-trips, sub ada
